+## Message wakes from a mediator (optional)
+
+A DIDComm mediator built on
+[didcomm-mediator-credo](https://github.com/openwallet-foundation/didcomm-mediator-credo)
+can POST `{"connectionId": …, "fcmToken": …}` to one URL
+(`PUSH_NOTIFICATIONS__WEBHOOK_URL`) for every message it queues for a
+recipient that is not connected. A device that wants those messages to ring it
+hands the mediator its **handle** as the RFC 0734 `set-device-info` token (never
+a push-service token), and asks its VTA to add the gateway's own DID to the
+handle's allowlist (`suggestedTriggers`).
+
+| Env | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `GATEWAY_MEDIATOR_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | Turns the route on. At least 32 characters of `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`; the mediator's URL is `https://<gateway>/mediator-webhook/<secret>` |
+| `GATEWAY_MESSAGE_WAKE_MIN_INTERVAL_SECS` | At most one message wake per handle per this many seconds (default 30) |
+
+The gateway then wakes the handle as **its own** trigger: a handle whose
+controller did not allowlist the gateway's DID is never rung this way, and a
+wrong secret answers `404`. Each message wake is interactive (shown, under the
+visible alert mode) and carries its own collapse id, `keyring-msg-<random>`,
+so a later message does not replace an earlier notification. That prefix tells
+the app, on a tap, that the wake was for a message; it also tells the push
+service that this wake was for a message rather than an approval.
+
+The answer is always `200` with a `status` (`delivered`, `ignored` for a
+missing token, `unknownHandle`, `notAllowed`, `rateLimited`,
+`transientFailure`, `tokenUnregistered`), except `404` for a wrong secret,
+`400` for a body that is not JSON, and `503` when the gateway has no DID. The
+secret is in the request path, so keep HTTP request logging at a level that
+does not print paths in production.
+
 ## Testing Web Push end-to-end
 
 The wake loop spans the gateway, a VTA + mediator, and the browser plugin. A
